hi there steve here again and uh once  again i'm going to talk about language  learning if you enjoy these videos  please subscribe  if you find this one useful please give  it a like  um today i want to talk about  noticing in language learning  now you've probably heard me say that  the three keys to language learning are  your attitude in other words  uh are you interested in the language do  you think you can do it um  are you um you know confident you can do  it um  do you think the method you're using is  gonna work all these attitude things are  extremely important  the second one is  oh and i should add on the attitude side  uh you have to be prepared to deal with  uh frustration uncertainty and so forth  uh the second one is time of course you  have to put in a lot of time we can't  learn overnight  and then the third one is the ability to  notice and a lot of people don't  understand what is meant by that  furthermore uh the idea that noticing is  an important part of language learning  is challenged in academic circles  challenged even by my great mentor  stephen krashen  so the reason i'm talking about this  today is that i noticed noticed  that in my turkish learning  that i seem to be going through sort of  a  an evolution in my ability to absorb  turkish so  initially in going through my mini  stories  really all i was doing was accumulating  vocabulary and i wasn't really paying  much attention to  the tenses  and  languages are a lot of them quite  redundant so you don't always have to  have the pronoun or you don't  necessarily always have to have all the  words or understand the grammar  in order to get a sense of what the  story is about by the same token if you  are using this content these stories for  language acquisition you can also deal  with the story being a little bit  less than clear  and that can be even true when i was  reading you know russian novels like  anna karenina and not really having a  good grasp of russian cases i was  sometimes not really clear as to who was  doing what to whom  but i'm accumulating words and and at  the same time accumulating sort of a  sense of the language  but now when i listen to these stories  for the umpteenth time or read them for  the umpteenth time  i find that i'm starting to concentrate  more on tenses  part of that was printing out  long lists of uh you know tenses in  turkish  reading through them once twice three  times four times not really  being able to remember anything  but it just made me that little bit more  attentive to the issue of tenses and so  now when i read these same stories where  i have the words i know it's  you know eat run say do  now i pay attention to whether it's will  run or did run and i  i start to see these patterns better i  start to notice  some aspects of the grammar better there  are other aspects that i'm still not  kind of comfortable with and the  noticing is is not just recognizing what  the tense is but also  starting to feel as if that structure  has that meaning  so you know for example if you see uh  the n ending in turkish it's like  apostrophe s that starts to become  natural so that the evolution is i pick  up a lot of vocabulary then i start to  notice certain things  either because i've seen them so often  or because i looked it up  or someone corrected me but for whatever  reason i start to notice these things  then i pay more attention to those  things  and i think that there is a natural  evolution there i think  in our brains we are our brains from an  evolutionary perspective are best at  sort of  sorting out patterns  based on a lot of stimulus  we are not quite as good at theoretical  explanations  uh we're not very good at block learning  tables  however some of these explanations and  some of these tables cause us to pay  more attention to some parts of the  language  and then we start to notice certain  things better and that helps us then  we notice them when we read i watch it  you know a  turkish movie on netflix and i noticed  something there again so that  it's a bit to me it's like  you know it's like a jigsaw puzzle when  you first start a jigsaw puzzle you got  nothing but as you start to put the  pieces together you start to notice  where some of these other pieces might  fit and i think the language learning is  like that it's an evolutionary  perspective  but obviously things that help you pay  attention to  some aspects of structure of  grammar and stuff help you notice if you  notice something  then you are more likely to to associate  that structure with  the tense or with other aspects you know  functional  meaning in a sentence so i think that  that noticing is important but if you  google  noticing in language learning you'll see  that there's a tremendous dispute  amongst  learned people in language acquisition  um i have to admit that i'm very  skeptical  of a lot of the research into language  acquisition although i have read  lots of books on the subject i have lots  of books here on the subject  but i think that that a lot of the you  know rather than these books on language  acquisition i prefer this book  on how the brain learns because i think  the learning of language is not so  different from learning other things  and the problem with a lot of the  research on language acquisition it's is  based on the the assumption that  learning takes place in a classroom so  that  whenever they do studies they study  learners in a classroom  i haven't  seen any studies where they deal  strictly with  independent language learners or people  who learn just  you know incidentally because they're in  a an environment where the language is  spoken sort of that non-formal  acquisition of languages crashing comes  closest to that because he points out  quite correctly in my view that the  most important thing is listening and  reading  you know just input  meaningful comprehensible hopefully  eventually compelling input lots of it i  don't know what my statistics are  uh in turkish i should have looked them  up but i'm sure i'm over 50 60 hours of  listening and however 50 60 000 words of  reading  there was a brief i had a brief twitter  exchange with someone who said that i  think someone asked what is the worst  advice you've ever gotten on language  learning and someone said well one was  to totally depend on grammar and the  other one was don't speak until you're  ready and i said why is that bad advice  i speak when i feel like speaking when i  feel i'm ready or i should add when i  have the opportunity like if i'm  learning turkish or farsi and i see  someone who looks like they might be  speaker of that language i'll try and  use it but otherwise i don't worry about  speaking i speak when i'm ready and then  this person came back and said no the  average person is  you know a perfectionist or is timid or  is reluctant  my experience is that if you have  acquired a strong level of comprehension  in the language  listening and reading you understand  what you hear  then the likelihood is that that person  is going to start speaking with  difficulty at first  if they have really good comprehension  skills because the biggest i think fear  people have is that they won't  understand what is said back to them but  if they have very good comprehension  skills the words and phrases are  bubbling in your mind you i think the  natural tendencies to want to try to use  them so i i think a lot of people  who study in the traditional sort of  language class where they are forced to  speak actually they don't end up  speaking that well and if they would put  more emphasis  on acquisition on on input on listening  and reading and developing that ability  to notice wanting to notice uh allowing  different things you know at different  times you notice different things  the first  many passes through my many stories i'm  just  noticing words and acquiring words but  after a while i start to be more curious  about these different forms and then i  start to notice the past tense the  future tense and i think later on i'll  notice some other aspects of turkish so  that the the ability to notice develops  gradually and there are a number of  things that contribute to it but it is  an important part of acquiring the  language thank you for listening bye for  now
 

